Respondent No.2 as approved by the Govt., the compensation of 80% (as is procedure in vogue) for the aforesaid land was received by all the land owners without any protest @ Rs. 2. 64 lacs per kanal plus 15% Jabrana except respondent No.1;
(ii) that respondent No.1 moved an application to respondent No.2 to the effect that a reference u/s 18 of Land Acquisition Act be moved to Pr. District Judge Pulwama,
which was forwarded to the reference court by respondent No.2, the said court (reference court of Pr. District Judge Pulwama) after the adjudication of the matter without impleading appellant as party but putting the appellant to notice, passed judgment and decree impugned which is against law and facts, as the reference court has relied on oral evidence without any documentary proof, the report of the proceedings is no-nest in the eyes of law, the reference court has come to the wrong conclusion by enhancing the compensation amount to Rs.
11.75 lacs per kanal plus 15% Jabrana with 6% interest from the date of filing of application for reference without any material on record;
(iii) that it is settled position of law that while assessing compensation, kind of soil, site and productivity is to be taken into consideration, in the present case the land in question is agricultural in nature having potential providing only one crop with 100% flood risk and falls outside the Municipal limits of Awantipora, the reference court without any material on record has burdened the appellant with huge liability on account of enhancement in compensation for the said land, none of the witnesses examined by the respondent No.1 has quoted any personal transaction with documentary proof to substantiate the rate, nor any registered sale deed has been provided to suggest the site value of the land in area;
(iv) that the reference court has awarded the compensation to respondent No.1 @ Rs. 11.75 lacs plus 15 % Jabrana per kanal with 6% interest from the date of application for reference fixing the rate of land as Rs. 11.75 lacs per kanal while considering the rate of land of another village namely Awantipora District Pulwama which is against law, as village Noorpora where the land of respondent No.1 has been acquired is far away from Awantipora and is outside the municipal limits of Awantipora

,is totally unfounded and not sustainable in the eyes of law because in village Awantipora acquisition proceedings in terms of section 11-B of J&K Land Acquisition Act has lapsed and the compensation for land has not been received by the land owners, that being the reasons a rate of Rs. 11.75 lacs in the year 2014 was fixed by PNC (Private Negotiation Committee) for Awantipora village, while in the case of Respondent No.1 the rate of his land at Noorpora has been fixed @ Rs. 2.64 lacs plus 15% Jabrana per kanal in the year 2007 which amount has been already received by other beneficiaries without any objection or protest who are similarly situated, this fact has not been appreciate by the reference court;
(v) that respondent No.1 has not led any evidence to support his claim against the award passed by respondent No.2, Honble Supreme Court from time to time has laid down guidelines for taking into consideration while determining the market value and the compensation of the land, no National Highway Authority Of India vs Ghulam Qadir Shah S/O Abdul Majeed Shah ... on 17 March, 2023 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/37957397/
2 evidence has been produced by respondent No.1 like comparative sales those could have taken place at the time of notification, there is nothing to show that there have been any bona-fide transaction in respect of any adjacent area to the land acquired, the reference court in absence of any evidence ought not to have altered the award passed by respondent No.2, the enhanced compensation arrived at by the reference court are totally unfounded based on no evidence;
(vi) that the claim of respondent No.1 that the land adjacent to his land at Awantipora has been assessed at Rs. 11.75 lacs per kanal is misleading on the ground that as per mandate of section 23 (1)
of the J&K Land Acquisition Act, the relevant date for the determination of market value of the land is the date on which notification u/s 6 of the said Act is issued, in the instant case the said notification No. 148-FC is of 2007, therefore, the rates of compensation were determined on the basis of market rates of the land prevalent on 14.08.2007, the tentative award in the present case was issued on 14.07.2008 vide No. DCP/LAS/NHW/06/N-1 541 while as final award came to be issue on 15-01-2014 in view of the delay caused due to the embezzlement of the funds in Collectorate which resulted in re-verification of land and issuance of corrigendums to reflect changes in the quantum of area/land, the land in village Awantipora where R-1 refers was acquired through private negotiations on 21-01-2014 and the rates were negotiated on the basis of market rates prevalent on the said date of 02-01-2014, besides the land in village Awantipora falls within Municipal areas,
therefore, the rates adopted in village Awantipora in 2014 cannot be made basis for deciding market rates in village Noorpora on 14.08.2007, these facts were brought into the notice of reference court by respondent No.2 by way of objections, but the same have not been considered, moreso, the application filed by R-1 u/s 18 of J&K Land Acquisition Act was time barred and the said aspect too has not been appreciated by the reference court.
